# ApexYard

> ApexYard gives your agents a team's process, then checks every pull request against it. The framework is open source (MIT).

<!-- feature-all:gate -->## Your agents build. The gate decides what ships.
<!-- /feature-all:gate -->
<!-- feature-off:gate --><!-- feature:build -->## Your agents build. Your rules guide every change.<!-- /feature:build -->
<!-- /feature-off:gate -->

ApexYard gives your agents a team's process, then checks every pull request against it.

Agent agnostic. Works with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode, Pi and omp.

<!-- feature:gate readiness -->## One process, three places

<!-- feature-all:gate readiness -->Keel is your team's rules and context. Build, the Gate and Readiness all work from it. A miss comes back as a new rule.
<!-- /feature-all:gate readiness -->
<!-- feature-off:gate readiness -->Keel holds your team's rules and context.
<!-- feature:build -->Build guides your agents with those rules.<!-- /feature:build -->
<!-- feature:gate -->Gate checks pull requests against them.<!-- /feature:gate -->
<!-- feature:readiness -->Readiness measures repositories against them.<!-- /feature:readiness -->
<!-- /feature-off:gate readiness -->

1. **Keel**: Every API endpoint checks who is calling. A rule your team adds to its handbook, marked blocking.
<!-- feature:build -->2. **Build**: Before an agent writes code, it loads your rules. It writes each new endpoint with an auth check.<!-- /feature:build -->
<!-- feature:gate -->3. **Gate**: Rex, the review agent, checks each pull request against the rule. A blocking rule that fails stops the merge.<!-- /feature:gate -->
<!-- feature:readiness -->4. **Readiness**: The readiness audit fails when a user-facing app has no authentication. On me2resh/ddd-sam-example, the top action was: add authentication to all patient appointment APIs.<!-- /feature:readiness -->
5. **A miss becomes a rule**: When a person finds a miss in review, /codify-rule turns the comment into a draft rule. You approve it before it is saved.

<!-- feature:buildpage -->- About Build: <https://apexyard.ai/build><!-- /feature:buildpage -->
<!-- feature:gate -->- About Gate: <https://apexyard.ai/gate><!-- /feature:gate -->
<!-- feature:readiness -->- About Readiness: <https://apexyard.ai/readiness><!-- /feature:readiness -->

Underneath: Keel, one evidence store and one dashboard.
<!-- /feature:gate readiness -->

20 roles, 69 skills, 23 agents and 66 hooks.

<!-- feature:gate -->Our servers never fetch your code. The GitHub App sees pull request titles, descriptions and changed file names, not the files. See what the GitHub App can see: <https://apexyard.ai/gate#app-access>.<!-- /feature:gate -->

<!-- feature:build -->## Install ApexYard<!-- /feature:build -->

Open source. MIT. Free to use.

<!-- feature:gate readiness pricing -->The hosted parts are paid. <!-- /feature:gate readiness pricing -->Every rule, hook and review agent is a plain file you can read, change or remove.

Install the command line tool, then create your private ops repository. The command asks for the name and the account:

```sh
brew install me2resh/tap/apexyard && apexyard init
```

For scripts, use the flag form in the [docs](/docs).

```sh
npx create-apexyard
```

